    </TIME_ORDER>
    <TIER LINGUISTIC_TYPE_REF="default-lt" TIER_ID="default"/>
    <TIER LINGUISTIC_TYPE_REF="imported-sep" TIER_ID="SPEAKER_00">
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a2"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts1" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts13">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Today, you're going to learn five killer tips to help improve your storytelling, and we'll be talking with Kelly D. Parker. If you have not yet met Kelly, you're going to today. She is a professional speaker.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a3"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts16" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts29">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>A TEDx speaker, and she works with companies and marketing departments and professionals to help them make their storytelling more memorable. She's also the host of a podcast called Business Storytelling Made Easy.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a4"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts32" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts43">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>I'll put all the details below so you can check out her podcast, her LinkedIn page, and her website as well. Kelly, how are you doing today?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a6"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts53" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts65">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>I'm so glad we finally got you on the channel. And you've come today to us with five great tips to improve our storytelling. So, why don't you hit us with that first tip and we'll jump right in.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a12"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts193" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts231">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>That's a great place to start because I know exactly what you mean. Yes, I go to church. Yes, I've seen guest speakers and everybody's holding their breath. Are they going to go on some long tangent or are they going to make a point? And I think sometimes we tell stories for the pure entertainment value of it. But if there's no point behind it for our listeners, if they're, you know, we don't know why we're communicating this message, it can really, it can really miss the mark. And so I think that's a great place to start. You have to know that point that you're driving at. And that becomes the focus of everything you say and a good way to make decisions about what to include and what to leave out. What's your second tip?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a16"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts319" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts355">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Right. I love that because, and by the way, I'll put a link to Kelly's TEDx talk below in the description below the video. But I love that because when a speaker does this and I'm in the audience, I'm listening, I feel like if they're focused on the problem that I have, they understand me. They understand what I'm trying to get in life or out of a situation and they're speaking directly to my issue. And it really makes me feel like they have a lot more credibility because they understood enough that I have a problem I want solved and they're going to speak directly to that problem. So I love that. What's your third tip?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a20"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts455" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts495">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, I love it. A lot of times people don't, speakers don't even think about the emotion. I worked with speakers who were very professional, buttoned up, and they delivered the content well, but the emotion was sort of flat and one-dimensional. And so I like how you talk about the emotional journey that we go on, because there might be different parts of the presentation or a message where there are ups and downs in the story. And you have to be aware as a speaker to bring them along in that journey think about it yeah you don't want to you don't want to be a downer if that's really not the main point of your of your talk you know you want to think about the emotions you want to create and convey. Wonderful. So in addition to emotions what's your fourth tip</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a26"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts603" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts629">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, enough detail so that they can picture the image in their head and they'll remember that story because of the concrete details. When you said at the beginning, the general description, oh, he liked to goof off, like it's hard to picture goofing off, but it's easier to picture an actual instance of him causing some trouble and the way his appearance. And I love that. That's great. Concrete details will really make that story pop. And what is your fifth tip?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a30"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts659" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts665">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>I like the fact that there's cheese in it.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a479"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts771" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts774">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a33"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts776" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts807">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Some contrast, some drama, some before and after. That transformation takes us on the emotional ups and downs versus just saying it straight and plain. I love the example of you could, for example, you could talk about someone who's a big success. That's an okay story. But if they came from nothing and then they became a big success, now there's some drama in that story. Now it gives us a reason to lean forward and be like, wow, how did this happen? It gives it, it's a hook to pay even more attention to this story. I love it.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a34"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts810" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts827">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>So I'm gonna put on the screen now your summary of your five tips. These are Kelly's five tips to make your messages, your stories much more memorable. So, these are the ways you want to think about any stories you tell in the future. Integrate these points, double-check your stories to make sure they do all five of these things.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a35"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts828" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts841">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Make sure you click on the links below to check out Kelly's podcast or TEDx talk or LinkedIn profile. Kelly, thank you so much for being here with us today.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a37"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts856" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts867">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>I agree. It was great to have you on. We'll have you on again soon. Thanks, everybody. Take care. See you soon.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
    </TIER>
